---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import ProteinCalculator from '@components/islands/ProteinCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'protein-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How much protein do I need per day?',
    answer:
      'It depends on your weight and goals. The general baseline is about 0.8 g per kg of body weight, rising to 1.2–1.6 g/kg for active people and athletes, and up to about 2.2 g/kg when building muscle or dieting to preserve it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I calculate my daily protein intake?',
    answer:
      'Multiply your body weight in kilograms by a target in grams per kg. A 70 kg (154 lb) recreationally active person at 1.4 g/kg needs about 98 g a day; the same person strength training at 1.8 g/kg needs about 126 g. To convert pounds to kilograms, divide by 2.205.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you eat too much protein?',
    answer:
      'For healthy people, protein well above the baseline is generally considered safe, though extremely high intakes offer no extra benefit and displace other nutrients. People with kidney disease should follow medical guidance, as they may need to limit protein.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use body weight or lean mass?',
    answer:
      'This calculator uses total body weight, which is the most common approach. People with very high body fat may prefer to base intake on lean or goal weight — treat the result as a practical estimate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What foods are high in protein?',
    answer:
      'Chicken, fish, lean beef, eggs, Greek yogurt, cottage cheese, milk, lentils, beans, tofu, tempeh and edamame are all protein-dense. Spreading these across meals — roughly 20–40 g each — helps your body use protein most effectively.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate your recommended daily protein intake based on your body weight and activity or goal, with a comparison across common targets."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-18' }}
>
  <ProteinCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>Metric</strong> or <strong>Imperial</strong> units and enter your <strong>body weight</strong>.</li>
      <li>Select the <strong>goal</strong> that matches your training and objective.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see your daily protein target, with a comparison across all the goals.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How protein needs scale</h2>
    <p>
      Protein requirements rise with body weight and with how hard you train. A sedentary adult
      needs only enough to maintain everyday tissue turnover; endurance athletes, people lifting to
      build muscle, and dieters trying to hold on to muscle all need more. The calculator expresses
      each goal as grams of protein per kilogram of body weight.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>Your target is simply your weight multiplied by a per-kilogram factor:</p>
    <p><strong>Protein (g) = body weight (kg) × factor</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>0.8 g/kg</strong> — sedentary, minimal exercise (the general baseline)</li>
      <li><strong>1.2 g/kg</strong> — active / general fitness</li>
      <li><strong>1.4 g/kg</strong> — endurance athlete</li>
      <li><strong>1.8 g/kg</strong> — strength training / muscle gain</li>
      <li><strong>2.2 g/kg</strong> — preserving muscle while dieting</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Working in pounds? Divide your weight by 2.205 to get kilograms first — or just enter imperial units and let the calculator convert.</p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>For a <strong>70 kg (154 lb)</strong> person, the goals work out to:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Goal</th><th>Factor</th><th>Daily protein</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Sedentary</td><td>0.8 g/kg</td><td>56 g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Active / general fitness</td><td>1.2 g/kg</td><td>84 g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Endurance athlete</td><td>1.4 g/kg</td><td>98 g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Strength / muscle gain</td><td>1.8 g/kg</td><td>126 g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Preserving muscle while dieting</td><td>2.2 g/kg</td><td>154 g</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Moving from recreational training (98 g) to a muscle-gain goal (126 g) adds under 30 g a day — roughly one extra chicken breast or a large scoop of whey.</p>

    <h2>Spreading it across the day</h2>
    <p>
      Your body uses protein most effectively when it is distributed rather than crammed into one
      meal. Splitting a target across three or four meals — very roughly 20–40 g each — is easier to
      digest and to hit consistently than a single large serving. Including some protein at each meal
      also helps with fullness.
    </p>

    <h2>High-protein foods</h2>
    <p>
      Protein-dense options include chicken, fish, lean beef, eggs, Greek yogurt, cottage cheese and
      milk, plus plant sources such as lentils, beans, tofu, tempeh and edamame. A varied mix covers
      the full range of amino acids, which matters most when plant foods make up the bulk of your
      intake.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pair your protein target with the <a href="/health/calorie-calculator">calorie calculator</a>
      and the <a href="/health/fat-intake-calculator">fat intake calculator</a> for a complete plan.
      These are general guidelines, not medical or dietary advice; people with kidney conditions
      should follow medical guidance, which may limit protein.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
